From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:09:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(crafting): expose Bedrock recipes and place ingredients

Display representable crafting-table recipes from Bedrock data with stable Java IDs and honor unlock events. Place ingredients through server-authoritative item stack requests, using tracked network IDs and recipe counts. Show a ghost recipe when ingredients are unavailable.

Manual crafting now resolves overlapping shapeless ingredients and recipe priority. Evaluate literal any_tag and all_tags Molang item queries against generated Bedrock item tags; leave unsupported expressions hidden. The server remains authoritative for the final craft.

Treat wildcard ingredient metadata as a wildcard when building Java recipe displays. Interpreting it as literal item metadata hid otherwise valid recipes.

Use the same placement logic for the player 2x2 grid so recipe book clicks work in both the player inventory and crafting table.

Batch shift crafting into one compatible inventory stack per request. Cap the recipe count by ingredient availability, stack capacity, and Bedrock action limits, then predict the same quantity locally until the server responds.

Include CraftResults immediately after CraftRecipe for both crafting-table
and player-grid requests. Geyser's InventoryTranslator requires this
transition before ingredient consumption and rejects a craft without it.
Keep each recipe result's per-craft amount separate from the batch count.

Verified against Geyser 2.11.3 source at
https://github.com/GeyserMC/Geyser/blob/63a4e2b79b12f0d138777d5fd80176a112b4bd72/core/src/main/java/org/geysermc/geyser/translator/inventory/InventoryTranslator.java
and focused request-order tests.

Keep transmitted block item metadata in both the recipe/result item codec
and the network stack descriptor. Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372,
protocol 2193, on CubeCraft Pillars of Fortune sends stripped oak wood
(runtime item ID -819) with aux 8; its appended plank recipe also requires
aux 8. Resetting that field to zero prevents the player-grid matcher from
selecting the advertised recipe. Block runtime-state validation remains
independent of item metadata. The wire-to-ingredient regression reproduces
8 becoming 0 before this change and matches a shifted player-grid ingredient
after it. Preserve the existing server-assigned recipe IDs and request path.

Expose protocol 2193 single-input furnace, blast-furnace and smoker recipes
in their own Java recipe-book categories, with station-specific stable
display IDs. Route recipe selection to ordinary Place actions on the
correct ingredient provider. Cooking and fuel/output updates remain server
owned. Combine compatible inventory stacks, preserve input variants, clear
mismatched input into existing stacks where possible, and plan atomically
before recording both inventories for acknowledgment or rejection recovery.
Use negative client request references when an action revisits a slot
changed earlier in the same request; never invent a server stack identity.
Reject recipes belonging to a different station.

Evidence: the pinned 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 recipe catalog advertises cooking
recipes as tagged ShapelessRecipe entries. The matching 1.26.51-beta.1
protocol metadata documents client request IDs in ItemStackNetIdVariant.
Java 26.3 FurnaceRecipeDisplay.STREAM_CODEC has input, fuel, result, station,
VarInt duration and float experience, and recipe display registry ID 2.
CraftingData carries neither time nor XP, so those Java display-only fields
stay neutral. They do not replace Bedrock ContainerSetData, native fuel
rules, or cooking simulation. The add-on's native cooking screens and
recipe controls still need implementation and native/route verification.

Geyser InventoryTranslator at dd10af11b5d8f8bcc396dde0f2751534aa3f4ad7
checks ordinary transfer identities against server inventory and accepts
negative request references. Targeted tests cover typed/direct recipe
encoding, category/unlock routing, partial stack aggregation, capacity,
variant selection, stale identities, provider mappings and rejected requests.

Retain protocol 2193 furnace options per family and register packet 351 in
both directions. Keep tab selection and opaque enum values in native
state, and project only layout/filter fields into Java recipe-book
settings. Preserve other books when the server updates one family.
Evidence: 1.26.51-beta.1 protocol 2193 FurnaceOptions metadata and
matching gophertunnel 80c811b6186016b3860c358368cfa47e507f26e9 agree on
byte family, signed compressed tab, boolean filter and signed compressed
layout. Java 26.3 RecipeBookSettings orders crafting, furnace, blast
furnace and smoker boolean pairs. This projection is a compatibility
path; native screen controls and route comparisons remain incomplete.

Transport the retained native furnace options through a separately
negotiated protocol-2193 payload in both directions. Replay all retained
families after registration or entering play, echo Java/server changes,
and advertise the return channel for Fabric clients on direct and ViaProxy
connections. Withdraw the capability after client unregistration. Preserve
unknown native enum values without forcing them into Java book state.
Decode bounded native envelopes before dispatch so malformed input cannot
mutate preferences or become a server edit. Native furnace scene controls
and native/direct/ViaProxy behavior comparisons remain incomplete.
